---
import BaseLayout from "@layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import LeadForm from "@components/LeadForm.astro";
import { BRAND } from "~/lib/brand";
import { telHref, smsHref, formatPhoneDisplay } from "~/lib/phone";
---

<BaseLayout
  seo={{
    title: "Emergency Roof Tarping & Storm Response",
    description:
      "Free emergency roof tarping if your roof is leaking right now, plus a same-day inspection and a forty-photo packet for your insurance claim. No deductible games.",
    pathname: "/storm-response",
  }}
>
  <section class="bg-[color:var(--color-navy-950)] text-white">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-16">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold tracking-widest text-[color:var(--color-gold-300)] uppercase">
        Storm response
      </p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl text-white md:text-6xl">Roof leaking right now? We tarp it free.</h1>
      <div class="rule-gold mt-6" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg">
        Emergency tarping and temporary dry-in at no charge, with no commitment to anything else.
        Then a same-day inspection and a forty-photo documentation packet for your insurance claim.
        No pressure, no door-knocking, no deductible games.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={telHref(BRAND.phoneE164)} data-cta-id="storm-call"
          >Call {formatPhoneDisplay(BRAND.phoneE164)}</a
        >
        <a
          class="btn btn-ghost-dark"
          href={smsHref(BRAND.phoneE164, "Storm hit — need inspection.")}
          data-cta-id="storm-sms">Text us</a
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl gap-10 px-4 py-12 md:grid-cols-[1fr_420px]">
    <article class="prose-northvale">
      <h2>What we do in the 48 hours after a storm</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Same-day roof inspection</strong> — photo every slope, ridge, valley, and penetration.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tarp + temporary dry-in</strong> if active leaking — free, no commitment to full repair.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Documentation packet</strong> — forty photos, hail/wind impact notes, and a claim-ready
          scope of loss.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Adjuster meet</strong> — we meet your adjuster on-site. You don't have to be there.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Written estimate + scope</strong> — matches the insurance carrier's Xactimate line items.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <h2>Emergency tarping, and what it actually does</h2>
      <p>
        A tarp is not a repair. It is a controlled way to stop water entering the house while
        everything else — the inspection, the claim, the scheduling of real work — happens on a sane
        timeline instead of at 11pm in the rain. We fasten it so it stays on through the next
        system, rather than draping a sheet over the ridge and weighting it with bricks, which is
        what most of the tarps we get called out to re-do look like.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>We photograph the damage before the tarp goes on.</strong> This is the part people get
        wrong, and it matters: once a tarp is down, the thing your adjuster needs to see is underneath
        it. Every homeowner policy carries a <em>Duties After Loss</em> section asking you to take reasonable
        steps to protect the property from further damage — read yours, it is a short numbered list in
        the policy booklet — and documenting what was there beforehand is what keeps a mitigation step
        from being mistaken for the loss itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        Tarping is free and carries no obligation to use us for the repair. If you call at midnight,
        get a tarp, and hire somebody else in the morning, that is a perfectly fine outcome. We
        would rather your house stayed dry.
      </p>
      <h3>When a tarp is not the answer</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Structural damage.</strong> A limb through the decking, or rafters that have moved,
          is a dry-in and shoring problem, not a tarp problem.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Active high wind.</strong> We will not put a crew on a roof mid-event. We come when
          it is survivable, which is usually sooner than you think.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Water already in the ceiling.</strong> Stopping the roof leak does not dry the structure.
          That is a restoration trade, and we will say so rather than pretend a tarp finished the job.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h2>What we will not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          We will not waive or absorb your deductible. Texas law requires you to pay it (Tex. Ins.
          Code § 707.002) and makes it an offense for a contractor to pay, waive, or rebate it (Tex.
          Bus. &amp; Com. Code § 27.02) — TDI warns of fines and jail time. Anyone offering to "eat
          it" is setting you up for a denied claim and criminal exposure.
        </li>
        <li>
          We will not door-knock or pressure a signature. TDI's storm guidance is blunt: it's
          against the law for out-of-town contractors to demand a down payment before starting after
          a disaster, and you should never sign a contract that assigns work "for the value of
          insurance proceeds." We leave a written estimate and let you decide on your schedule.
        </li>
        <li>
          We will not act as a public adjuster. Texas Insurance Code § 4102.163 prohibits a roofing
          contractor from adjusting a claim on the same property. We're the contractor.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Once the emergency is handled</h2>
      <p>
        This page is for the day the storm hits. For the full picture — what hail and wind damage
        look like, the Texas claim deadlines your insurer must meet, and how the process runs end to
        end — read <a href="/services/storm-damage">the storm damage guide</a> and
        <a href="/services/insurance-claims"> the insurance claims guide</a>. In The Woodlands?
        <a href="/the-woodlands/storm-damage"> Our local storm page</a> covers this area's actual storm
        record.
      </p>
    </article>
    <aside>
      <LeadForm source="storm-response" defaultService="storm" />
    </aside>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
